\subsection{Riccati interpretation of the $S=0$ regularizer}
\label{sec:rs-riccati-analysis}

The scale $\sigma_{aw}\tau^3$ describes the coordinate $S$ but does not
determine the covariance of the $S=0$ pseudo-measurement.  Regularization
strength depends on predicted covariance, Kalman gain, and update cadence.  The
scalar reduction below makes these dependencies explicit.

\subsubsection{Dimensionless periodic Riccati problem}

For one translational axis, omit attitude and bias cross-couplings and use
\begin{equation}
 x=\begin{bmatrix}v&p&S&a\end{bmatrix}^{\!T},\qquad
 \dot v=a,\quad \dot p=v,\quad \dot S=p,
\label{eq:riccati-four-state-chain}
\end{equation}
with OU acceleration
\begin{equation}
 da=-\frac{1}{\tau}a\,dt
 +\sqrt{\frac{2\sigma_a^2}{\tau}}\,dW_t .
\label{eq:riccati-ou-accel}
\end{equation}
Define
\begin{equation}
 D=\operatorname{diag}(\sigma_a\tau,\,\sigma_a\tau^2,\,
                       \sigma_a\tau^3,\,\sigma_a),
 \qquad x=D\bar x,
\label{eq:riccati-D}
\end{equation}
and dimensionless time $u=t/\tau$.  For
$\delta=\Delta t/\tau$, exact propagation has normalized transition
\begin{equation}
\bar\Phi(\delta)=
\begin{bmatrix}
1&0&0&1-e^{-\delta}\\
\delta&1&0&\delta-1+e^{-\delta}\\
\frac12\delta^2&\delta&1&\frac12\delta^2-\delta+1-e^{-\delta}\\
0&0&0&e^{-\delta}
\end{bmatrix},
\label{eq:riccati-phibar}
\end{equation}
so that $\Phi=D\bar\Phi D^{-1}$ and $Q_d=D\bar QD$.
With scalar acceleration-measurement variance $R_a$ and integral
pseudo-measurement variance $R_S$, define
\begin{equation}
 \beta=\frac{R_a}{\sigma_a^2},\qquad
 \rho=\frac{T_S}{\tau},\qquad
 r=\frac{R_S}{\sigma_a^2\tau^6}.
\label{eq:riccati-rho-r}
\end{equation}
The periodic steady covariance therefore has the exact similarity form
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
 P_\star=D\,
 \mathcal F\!\left(
 \frac{\Delta t}{\tau},
 \frac{T_S}{\tau},
 \frac{R_a}{\sigma_a^2},
 \frac{R_S}{\sigma_a^2\tau^6}
 \right)D .}
\label{eq:riccati-four-groups}
\end{equation}
Thus holding $R_S/(\sigma_a^2\tau^6)$ constant preserves only one of four
dimensionless groups; fixed sample time, pseudo-update cadence, and
accelerometer noise prevent cubic coordinate scaling from defining a complete
closed-loop invariance law.

\subsubsection{Gain scaling and the role of the pseudo-measurement}

Immediately before a scalar pseudo-update,
\begin{equation}
 K_S=\frac{P^-e_S}{P^-_{SS}+R_S},
 \qquad e_S=\begin{bmatrix}0&0&1&0\end{bmatrix}^{T}.
\label{eq:riccati-full-S-gain}
\end{equation}
The direct contraction of $S$ is
\begin{equation}
 K_{SS}=\frac{P^-_{SS}}{P^-_{SS}+R_S}
       =\frac{\bar P^-_{SS}}{\bar P^-_{SS}+r},
\label{eq:riccati-KSS}
\end{equation}
and the displacement correction is
\begin{equation}
 \Delta p=-\frac{P^-_{pS}}{P^-_{SS}+R_S}S^- .
\label{eq:riccati-deltap}
\end{equation}
Since $P_{pS}=\sigma_a^2\tau^5\bar P_{pS}$ and
$S=\sigma_a\tau^3\bar S$, the displacement correction has natural scale
$\sigma_a\tau^2$.  The pseudo-measurement therefore acts through the full
cross-covariance rather than directly clamping position.

For prescribed scalar contraction $\gamma_S=K_{SS}$,
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
 R_S=\frac{1-\gamma_S}{\gamma_S}P^-_{SS}.}
\label{eq:riccati-gain-target}
\end{equation}
Using \eqref{eq:riccati-gain-target} would make $R_S$ depend on the filter's own
predicted covariance and therefore defines a different design from the
exogenous scheduler studied here.  It nevertheless shows that regularization
authority is a gain property, not a dimensional property of $S$ alone.

\subsubsection{Posterior low-frequency acceleration error}
\label{sec:riccati-accel-reduction}

A second reduction identifies the acceleration uncertainty driving the slow
integrator chain.  Write
\begin{equation}
 \dot a=-\lambda a+w,\qquad
 \lambda=\frac1\tau,\qquad
 q=\frac{2\sigma_a^2}{\tau},
\end{equation}
and approximate the high-rate sampled accelerometer by continuous measurement
$y_a=a+n_a$ with white-noise density $r_a$.  The scalar continuous Riccati
equation is
\begin{equation}
 0=-2\lambda P_a+q-\frac{P_a^2}{r_a},
\end{equation}
with stabilizing solution
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
 P_a=r_a(\mu-\lambda),\qquad
 \mu=\sqrt{\lambda^2+\frac{q}{r_a}}
 =\sqrt{\frac1{\tau^2}+\frac{2\sigma_a^2}{\tau r_a}}.}
\label{eq:riccati-accel-solution}
\end{equation}
The posterior acceleration-error PSD is
\begin{equation}
 S_{e_a}(\omega)=\frac{2P_a\mu}{\mu^2+\omega^2},
\end{equation}
with zero-frequency intensity
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
 q_{\mathrm{eff}}:=S_{e_a}(0)
 =\frac{2P_a}{\mu}
 =2r_a\left(1-\frac{1}{\mu\tau}\right).}
\label{eq:riccati-qeff}
\end{equation}
This reduced-model disturbance is distinct from the unconditional OU variance
$\sigma_a^2$.  In the full MEKF, attitude/gravity leakage, residual
accelerometer bias, and cross-covariances make its effective counterpart
state- and sea-dependent.

\subsubsection{Closed-form drift-band regularization bandwidth}
\label{sec:riccati-drift-band}

At frequencies well below $\mu$, approximate the residual acceleration error as
white noise of intensity $q_{\mathrm{eff}}$ and retain
$x_d=[S,p,v]^T$.  A pseudo-measurement of variance $R_S$ every $T_S$ has the
high-rate information-equivalent continuous density
\begin{equation}
 r_{S,c}=R_ST_S.
\label{eq:riccati-rsc}
\end{equation}
The corresponding continuous triple-integrator Riccati equation has gain
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
 K_d=\begin{bmatrix}2\omega_R&2\omega_R^2&\omega_R^3\end{bmatrix}^{T},
 \qquad
 \omega_R=\left(\frac{q_{\mathrm{eff}}}{R_ST_S}\right)^{1/6}.}
\label{eq:riccati-omegaR}
\end{equation}
Its characteristic polynomial is
\begin{equation}
(s+\omega_R)(s^2+\omega_Rs+\omega_R^2),
\end{equation}
so $R_S$ and $T_S$ jointly set the low-frequency correction bandwidth:
stronger or more frequent updates increase it, whereas larger $R_S$ decreases
it.

If a reduced design prescribes $\omega_R=\kappa/\tau$, then
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
 R_S=\frac{q_{\mathrm{eff}}\tau^6}{T_S\kappa^6}.}
\label{eq:riccati-pole-target-RS}
\end{equation}
For similarity-scaled cadence
\begin{equation}
\boxed{T_S=c_T\tau,}
\label{eq:riccati-scaled-TS}
\end{equation}
this becomes
\begin{equation}
 R_S=\frac{q_{\mathrm{eff}}\tau^5}{c_T\kappa^6}.
\label{eq:riccati-scaled-cadence-law}
\end{equation}
Two asymptotic limits show why coordinate similarity alone does not imply a
single exponent.  For weak acceleration observation,
$q_{\mathrm{eff}}\simeq2\sigma_a^2\tau$ and
\begin{equation}
 r_S\propto\sigma_a\tau^3.
\label{eq:riccati-scaled-cadence-weak}
\end{equation}
For strong observation with sea-amplitude-independent effective measurement
density, $q_{\mathrm{eff}}\simeq2r_a$ and
\begin{equation}
 r_S\propto\tau^{5/2}.
\label{eq:riccati-scaled-cadence-strong}
\end{equation}
These pole-preserving scalar limits omit distortion of real wave motion by the
false zero observation and therefore do not determine the complete-MEKF
amplitude dependence.

\subsubsection{Scope of the reduction}
\label{sec:riccati-scaled-cadence}

The four-group similarity result is exact for the scalar periodic covariance
recursion; $q_{\mathrm{eff}}$ and $\omega_R$ additionally invoke continuous
high-rate and low-frequency approximations.  Because these reductions omit
attitude/bias coupling, reset events, nonstationarity, and rejected wave energy,
they identify mechanisms and scaling variables rather than a full-MEKF tuning
law.  The next section adds wave distortion, and Sec.~\ref{sec:adaptation}
documents the applied empirical schedule.
